  "summary": "@tekmidian/pai: static keyword co-occurrence in bundled CLI without traced installer harm",
  "details": "The package ships a bundled Node CLI under dist/ composed of multiple minified.mjs chunks (cli, cli2, hooks/worker-*, routing, project-setup, local-tiers, models-add, session-scan, hang-probe, config). Keyword co-occurrence rules match on tokens such as `PATH`, `ping`, `curl`, `GET`, `POST`, `Buffer.from(..., 'base64').toString(...)`, and example-domain strings (`curl https://example.com`, `curl http://localhost`, `curl http://x`) inside the bundles and in src/hooks/ts/lib/rules/builtin.generated.ts, which is a rules file enumerating example commands as inert data. The matches are spread across unrelated chunks and lines, consistent with a developer-agent/CLI codebase that references shell commands as sample inputs, rule patterns, and process-launching utilities rather than a hardcoded exfiltration path. No install or import lifecycle script is declared that would auto-run these paths, no attacker-controlled destination domain or IP is bound to a network sink, no read of installer-secret paths (~/.aws, ~/.ssh, ~/.npmrc, browser or wallet stores) is tied to an outbound request, and the base64 decode sites are not wired to eval/exec of the decoded bytes. The observed signals are pattern co-occurrence in a minified bundle without a concrete installer-harm flow.",
